children.push(H1("2. Distributed Biosynthesized Energy Storage"));

children.push(H3("2.1 Rationale for Storage Infrastructure"));
children.push(P("The four-source generation architecture specified in §1 produces variable output across time and operational context. Without storage, the architecture operates live on whatever current production provides, with no buffer for transient mismatches between generation and demand. This produces three distinct failure modes that storage infrastructure addresses:"));

children.push(mix([{ text: "Surge mismatch. ", bold: true }, "Wireless charging delivered at rates exceeding instantaneous substrate consumption produces excess power that has nowhere to go. Without a sink, this excess dissipates as heat in the receiver coils and surrounding tissue. The thermal safety implications are addressed in §2.5; the immediate architectural response is that a capacitive or pseudocapacitive sink is required to absorb surge power before it produces local heating."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Demand spike fragility. ", bold: true }, "During active periods, computational or signaling demands may transiently exceed piezoelectric output. Without reserve capacity, these spikes either fail to be served or force the substrate into emergency throttling. Buffer storage at processing nodes allows transient spikes to be served from local reserve while generation catches up."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Charging discontinuity. ", bold: true }, "Periods between charging sessions, particularly during travel or environments without charging infrastructure, require operation from stored energy. Without deep storage capacity, the substrate falls onto mitochondrial up-regulation immediately upon disconnection. With deep storage, mitochondrial up-regulation is reserved for true emergency conditions and the host is spared the metabolic cost during routine disconnection."]));

children.push(P("The architecture therefore specifies storage at three tiers \u2014 fast capacitive, intermediate pseudocapacitive, and deep redox-protein \u2014 each fitted to a specific role. All three tiers are deployed through the biosynthetic infrastructure specified in Addendum I §3, with materials produced by host cells from trace dietary inputs and tagged self at formation."));

children.push(H3("2.2 Tier 1: Capacitive Storage via Biomineralized Dielectric Structures"));

children.push(H4("2.2.1 Dielectric Foundation"));
children.push(P("Hydroxyapatite (HA), the primary mineral component of bone tissue (Ca\u2081\u2080(PO\u2084)\u2086(OH)\u2082), has well-characterized dielectric properties relevant to capacitor function. Comprehensive review of HA's electrical and dielectric properties documents impedance, AC conductivity, dielectric constant, dielectric loss, and permittivity across powders, pellets, and thin films, with particular attention to dopant effects [Ref I1]. The dielectric constant of dry bone is comparable to that of sintered HA, and through control of density and dopants the dielectric constant of synthesized HA can be tailored to match natural bone or tuned for specific dielectric applications [Ref I1]. HA's high dielectric constant has been characterized in detail for biomedical contexts [Ref I2], with the porosity dependence of the dielectric constant of sintered HA documented as a function of microstructure [Ref I3]. Studies on HA-polymer composites, including HA-starch and chitosan-HA systems, demonstrate that dielectric properties can be tuned through composite formulation while maintaining biocompatibility and biodegradation profiles [Ref I4]. Discovery of piezoelectric and ferroelectric behavior in HA, characterized in Ca\u2081\u2080(PO\u2084)\u2086(OH)\u2082-Ba\u2080.\u2085Sr\u2080.\u2085TiO\u2083 composites designed for tissue engineering, opens applications as electrets in addition to its established bone tissue engineering role [Ref I5]."));
children.push(P("The architectural application is direct. Capacitors are fundamentally two conductors separated by a dielectric. The biosynthetic deployment infrastructure of Addendum I §3 produces both required components. Hydroxyapatite as the dielectric is produced by the same osteoblast-class cells that lay down normal bone matrix, with deposition geometry directed by AI command at thin-film thicknesses appropriate for capacitor function. Conductive layers (gold, copper, or alloyed metal nanoparticles, sequestered through ferritin-class proteins per Addendum I refs C11, C14) are deposited as parallel conductive sheets separated by the HA dielectric film."));

children.push(H4("2.2.2 Performance and Architecture"));
children.push(P("Tier 1 capacitive storage provides fast charge/discharge cycles, indefinite cycle life, and no chemistry-related failure modes. Energy density is lower than chemical batteries \u2014 capacitive storage is fundamentally limited by the dielectric breakdown strength times the capacitance \u2014 but the function being served is buffer and surge handling, not bulk energy storage. Distributed across the skeletal substrate at receiver nodes and processing nodes, Tier 1 capacitors absorb surge mismatches at sub-millisecond timescales."));
children.push(P("The architectural placement is determined by the function. Receiver-adjacent capacitors handle surge mismatch from wireless charging input. Processing-node capacitors handle demand spikes from local computation and sensor readout. Storage tier 1 does not serve as primary reserve; it serves as fast buffer."));

children.push(H3("2.3 Tier 2: Pseudocapacitive Storage via Iron Oxide and Related Metal Oxide Structures"));

children.push(H4("2.3.1 Pseudocapacitor Foundation"));
children.push(P("Pseudocapacitors achieve higher energy density than pure capacitors through reversible faradaic redox reactions at metal oxide interfaces while retaining capacitor-like fast charge/discharge characteristics [Ref J1, J2]. Iron-based oxides have been extensively characterized as pseudocapacitor materials due to multiple oxidation states of iron, rich redox chemistry, low toxicity, and earth abundance [Ref J3]. Reported specific capacitances span a wide range depending on synthesis and structure: cubic-phase magnetite (Fe\u2083O\u2084) nanoparticles synthesized by co-precipitation achieve 126 F/g at 2.5 A/g discharge [Ref J4]; FeOx on radially-oriented carbon nanofibers achieves 239.2 F/g via Fe(II)/Fe(III) redox [Ref J5]; mixed-valence iron oxide with carbon black grafting achieves 408 mF/cm\u00B2 (128 F/g) by single-step thermal annealing [Ref J6]; magnetic CuFe\u2082O\u2084 pseudocapacitors achieve 2.76 F/cm\u00B2 with magnetocapacitive effects [Ref J7]; Fe\u2083O\u2084 nanowires reach 442 F/g when integrated with reduced graphene oxide [Ref J3]. The progression of iron oxide nanostructures for energy storage is reviewed across Fe\u2081\u208B\u2093O, Fe\u2082O\u2083, and Fe\u2083O\u2084 phases [Ref J8]."));

children.push(H4("2.3.2 Architectural Integration With Biosynthesis"));
children.push(P("The iron oxide pseudocapacitor literature integrates directly with the ferritin-templated metal biomineralization specified in Addendum I §3. Recombinant ferritin's role in iron biomineralization is well-established [Addendum I refs C11, C14], and the biomineralized iron oxide cores produced by ferritin are precisely the Fe\u2083O\u2084, Fe\u2082O\u2083, and FeOOH phases that the pseudocapacitor literature characterizes as redox-active electrodes [Ref J3]. Ferritin protein cages have been used as templates for ferrimagnetic iron oxide nanoparticle synthesis with characterized morphology and magnetic properties [Ref K1, K2], and iron and cobalt oxide nanoparticles in the 20\u201360 \u00C5 range have been produced from ferritin templates with photoelectron spectroscopy and atomic force microscopy characterization [Ref K3]. Magnetoferritin synthesized using thermophilic archaeon ferritin templates at elevated temperature produces particles with iron loading up to 9,517 atoms per cage [Ref K4]."));
children.push(P("The architectural commitment is therefore: the same ferritin-class biosynthetic machinery that Addendum I specifies for iron sequestration produces, with appropriate AI-directed geometry control, iron oxide deposits that function as pseudocapacitor electrodes. This is presented as a proposed mechanism for the architectural integration. The constituent biology (ferritin-templated iron oxide synthesis) and the constituent electrochemistry (iron oxide pseudocapacitor function) are independently established. The combined deployment as an integrated biosynthesized pseudocapacitor in mammalian tissue under AI control has no peer-reviewed experimental precedent."));

children.push(H4("2.3.3 Tier 2 Architecture"));
children.push(P("Pseudocapacitive storage at processing nodes provides intermediate-scale buffer between Tier 1 fast surge handling and Tier 3 deep reserve. Charge/discharge timescales are slower than Tier 1 (milliseconds to seconds rather than microseconds) but energy density is substantially higher. The function served is: bridging extended demand spikes, buffering between charging sessions during normal operation, and providing reserve capacity that draws down only during prolonged disconnection from charging infrastructure."));

children.push(H3("2.4 Tier 3: Deep Storage via Redox-Active Protein Arrays"));

children.push(H4("2.4.1 Multi-Heme Cytochrome Conductivity Foundation"));
children.push(mix([
  Pt("Multi-heme cytochromes (MHCs) have been characterized as exceptional bioelectronic conductors. When placed in solid-state electronic junctions, MHCs support temperature-independent currents over several nanometers that are three orders of magnitude higher than other redox proteins of comparable size [Ref L1, L2]. The conduction mechanism in dry bioelectronic junctions has been characterized as off-resonant coherent tunneling mediated by protein valence-band orbitals strongly delocalized over heme and protein residues [Ref L1]. In aqueous environments, native MHC electron transfer occurs through consecutive heme-to-heme electron hopping [Ref L1, L3]. The structural and functional basis of MHC conductivity has been characterized across multiple species, including "),
  Pt("Shewanella oneidensis", { italics: true }),
  Pt(" (MtrCAB and related cytochromes) and "),
  Pt("Geobacter sulfurreducens", { italics: true }),
  Pt(" (OmcS, OmcZ, and related proteins) [Ref L1, L4]."),
]));
children.push(P("Cytochrome c (CytC) has been demonstrated as a bias-induced current switch in solid-state protein junctions, with reversible bias-controlled switching of protein ensembles persisting at room temperature [Ref L5]. Cytochrome b562 engineered with surface cysteine residues for direct gold self-assembly demonstrates reversible fast electron exchange over 20 \u00C5 between heme redox center and gold substrate, establishing long-range electron-transfer pathways [Ref L6]. Electron transfer at the interface between multi-heme cytochromes and metal oxide nanomaterials has been characterized at the molecular level with site-directed mutagenesis tunability [Ref L7]."));

children.push(H4("2.4.2 Tier 3 Architectural Application"));
children.push(P("The architectural commitment for Tier 3 storage is that MHC and related redox-protein arrays, deposited through the biosynthetic infrastructure of Addendum I §3 in structured arrays at high-volume bone marrow sites (long bones, pelvis), serve as deep storage that holds energy in reversible redox states. The biology that the architecture draws on is the same biology that mitochondria use for chemical energy storage, scaled up to a structural rather than purely metabolic role and integrated with the substrate's electrical network."));
children.push(P("This is presented as a proposed mechanism with significant architectural specification still required. The constituent biology \u2014 MHC bioelectronic conductivity, cytochrome c switching, cytochrome b562 long-range transfer \u2014 is established. Integration of these components into structured arrays for deep energy storage in mammalian tissue is not in the literature and requires substantial development before deployment."));
children.push(P("Energy density of redox-protein deep storage is intermediate between pseudocapacitive storage and chemical batteries. The advantages over chemical batteries are biocompatibility (fully biological materials with no toxic electrolyte), regenerability (depleted proteins can be replaced through normal cellular protein turnover under AI direction), and integration with the architecture's existing biological infrastructure."));

children.push(H3("2.5 Thermal Safety Architecture"));

children.push(H4("2.5.1 Bioheat Foundation"));
children.push(P("Heat dissipation in tissue is governed by the Pennes bioheat equation, which relates thermal conduction, metabolic heat generation, and heat transport by blood perfusion [Ref M1, M2]. The Pennes formulation includes thermal inertia, conduction, metabolic heat generation, and a perfusion term that captures heat removal by blood flow as proportional to the temperature difference between arterial blood entering tissue capillaries and venous blood leaving [Ref M1]. The model has been used extensively for medical device thermal safety evaluation, including implantable medical device heat dissipation [Ref M3]. Refinements address the limitations of the original Pennes assumption of uniform isotropic perfusion through heterogeneous-perfusion modifications [Ref M4], hyperbolic formulations that incorporate finite thermal relaxation [Ref M5], and porous-media approaches for high-perfusion contexts [Ref M6]. The competition between heat spreading via conduction and heat removal by blood perfusion determines local thermal lesion size, with the heat-sink effect near large vessels providing additional cooling capacity [Ref M3]."));
children.push(P("For the Braided Mind architecture, the relevant prediction is that local heating from a power dissipation event is limited by both conduction to surrounding tissue and convective cooling by blood perfusion. Bone marrow has high perfusion relative to cortical bone, providing substantial thermal sink capacity at receiver-adjacent sites. The architecture exploits this by routing dissipation events to highly-perfused tissue regions where the bioheat equation predicts rapid thermal equilibration with the bloodstream rather than local accumulation."));

children.push(H4("2.5.2 Wireless Charging Thermal Failure Mode Analysis"));
children.push(P("The wireless charging source specified in §1.3 introduces a controllable energy input from outside the host. Without storage and thermal safety architecture, mismatches between transmitter output and substrate consumption produce dissipation in receiver-adjacent tissue. The local heating risk is established by the implant SAR literature, which documents local SAR values up to 24.6 W/kg with associated 4.22\u00B0C temperature elevation in metallic implant regions during MRI scanning [§1 Ref D7]. Sustained local heating of 2\u00B0C and above produces tissue damage in time-dependent fashion characterized by Arrhenius models [Ref M7]."));
children.push(P("The architecture's response is layered:"));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Receiver-adjacent capacitive sinks (Tier 1). ", bold: true }, "Excess wireless charging power is shunted to nearby capacitive storage before it dissipates as heat. The fast charge time of capacitive storage means surge events fill capacitors faster than they produce thermal accumulation. Capacitor-saturation conditions trigger the next layer."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Detuning under fault conditions. ", bold: true }, "Resonant receiver coils can be detuned from the transmitter frequency under AI command, dropping coupling efficiency to near zero. When capacitive storage saturates and pseudocapacitive storage is also approaching capacity, the AI directs detuning, halting wireless power input regardless of transmitter behavior. Implementation as a switchable shunt that changes coil resonant frequency is controllable through expression of relevant ion channels or piezoelectric actuators in receiver-proximal cells."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Thermal monitoring at every receiver node. ", bold: true }, "Channel A2 mechanical telemetry (Addendum I §1.2) extends naturally to thermal sensing because piezoelectric properties of bone are temperature-dependent. The substrate already detects its own thermal state through the same readout mechanism that decodes mechanical input. Anomalous heating at receiver nodes triggers detuning, capacitor discharge to thermal-buffer regions, or full charging shutdown depending on severity."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Isolation of high-current paths from neural tissue. ", bold: true }, "Charging coils are placed at sites distant from sensitive neural structures (§1.3.3). The petrous bone primary node does not host charging coils; power transmitted to the petrous node arrives after voltage conditioning and at low currents that do not produce significant local heating."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Authenticated handshake for charging. ", bold: true }, "The receiver only accepts power from transmitters that complete a cryptographic handshake. Hostile or malformed input fields are rejected at the receiver level rather than absorbed and dissipated. The handshake operates over a low-bandwidth side channel separate from the power transfer itself."]));
children.push(P("Together these layers convert wireless charging from an architectural hazard into a controlled energy input with documented failure mode behaviors. The thermal safety architecture is non-optional in the deployment; receiver coils cannot be deployed without the corresponding capacitive sinks, detuning capability, thermal monitoring, and handshake protocol."));

children.push(H4("2.5.3 Operational Thermal Limits"));
children.push(P("The architecture commits to local temperature elevation limits below documented tissue damage thresholds. Sustained elevations above 2\u00B0C trigger detuning. Transient elevations during surge events are bounded by capacitor charge time and perfusion-mediated cooling. The specific threshold values require per-host calibration against individual perfusion characteristics and receiver placement, identified as an open parameter requiring empirical determination prior to clinical deployment of the wireless charging architecture."));

