children.push(H1("3. Operational Floor Analysis"));

children.push(H3("3.1 Rationale and Scope"));
children.push(P("The parent document (v3.2) and Addendum I treat failure modes implicitly. Specific failure cases are noted in §9 of v3.2 (e.g., HO-1 supplementation threshold as open parameter, packaging limits) and in Addendum I §2.4 (failure-mode analysis of the biocompatibility inversion), but the architecture as published does not specify a stratified analysis of operational state degradation, the points at which different system components fail, or the documented behaviors that should obtain at each degradation level."));
children.push(P("This section specifies five operational floors representing distinct degradation thresholds. Each floor is defined by which system functions remain available at that level, what triggers transition to the next floor, and what recovery requires. The principle drawn from safety-critical engineering is that complex systems require explicit failure-mode specification to be defensibly safe; ad-hoc failure handling in safety-critical embedded systems is identified as a negligent design omission [Ref N1, N2]. The Braided Mind architecture, operating at substantially higher complexity than conventional implantable medical devices, requires correspondingly more explicit operational-state analysis."));

children.push(H3("3.2 Floor 1: Storage Exhaustion"));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Definition. ", bold: true }, "Capacitive storage (Tier 1) and pseudocapacitive storage (Tier 2) drained or near-drained. Redox-protein deep storage (Tier 3) engaged and drawing down. The system is operating live on whatever current production from §1's four sources provides, with minimal buffer for transient mismatches between generation and demand."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Detection. ", bold: true }, "Storage state telemetry through the readout layer specified in §2 returns charge values approaching zero across Tier 1 and Tier 2. Channel A2 mechanical telemetry (Addendum I §1.2) registers normal operation; the substrate is not yet failing, only operating without buffer."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Behavior. ", bold: true }, "Single failure modes that would normally be absorbed by storage become survivable only if production sources remain stable. Transient spikes in demand, brief drops in piezoelectric output during posture changes, or short charging-field interruptions can no longer be smoothed and produce visible operational hiccups. The AI begins prioritizing energy conservation: defers non-essential computation, reduces sensor channel sampling rates to minimum required for safety monitoring, and prepares to escalate to Floor 2 if any production source falters."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Recovery. ", bold: true }, "Any sustained input restores Tier 1 and Tier 2 charge levels rapidly. Return to charging infrastructure is the most direct recovery path, providing surplus power that fills capacitive and pseudocapacitive storage in seconds to minutes. Increased mechanical activity restores piezoelectric output and provides modest charging. Increased caloric intake without metabolic up-regulation supports baseline operation."]));

children.push(H3("3.3 Floor 2: Production Deficit"));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Definition. ", bold: true }, "Combined output from all available power sources falling below operational baseline. Storage continues drawing down. The deficit is sustained rather than transient."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Detection. ", bold: true }, "Storage state monitoring shows Tier 3 charge declining over time despite source A (piezoelectric) or source B (wireless) activity. Channel A2 telemetry shows reduced piezoelectric production, indicating low mechanical loading. Wireless charging input absent or insufficient."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Behavior. ", bold: true }, "The AI throttles non-essential computation, suspends sensor channels not required for survival monitoring, reduces communication bandwidth to minimum sufficient for substrate health management. Storage continues to drain, but at a rate slowed by reduced consumption. The host may not be aware that the substrate is in degraded operation; the experiential change is minimal at this floor because the AI is prioritizing invisibility of its own constraint."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Recovery. ", bold: true }, "Restoration of any production source reverses the trajectory. The longer the state persists, the lower Tier 3 reserve falls, and the more quickly recovery must occur to avoid escalation to Floor 3."]));

children.push(H3("3.4 Floor 3: Mitochondrial Limit Reached"));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Definition. ", bold: true }, "Sustained metabolic up-regulation through the §1.5 pathway has been engaged for a duration approaching the host-specific safe limit. Continuing to draw on host metabolism becomes pathological."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Detection. ", bold: true }, "The AI tracks duration of mitochondrial source engagement against the host-specific calibration parameter identified in §1.5.5. Channel A1 telemetry (Addendum I §1.2) provides cytokine-level confirmation of sustained up-regulation through monitoring of leptin, FGF21, and related signaling. Host physiological state markers \u2014 elevated heart rate, elevated body temperature, increased respiratory rate \u2014 provide cross-channel confirmation."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Pathological consequences of sustained hypermetabolism. ", bold: true }, "Hypermetabolism is defined as elevated resting energy expenditure greater than 110% of predicted REE [Ref O1] and is associated with multiple end-organ consequences when sustained. Hyperthyroidism, the most common pathological hypermetabolic state, produces tachycardia, palpitations, dyspnea on exertion, increased cardiac output and contractility, decreased peripheral resistance, systolic hypertension, and widening pulse pressure [Ref O2]. The hyperthyroid state increases metabolic demand on the heart such that the heart may not withstand the increased oxygen demand, producing cardiac dysrhythmias, atrial fibrillation, and heart failure in advanced cases [Ref O2, O3]. Mild anemia, thrombocytopenia, hypercalcemia, muscle wasting, and bone loss occur frequently in hyperthyroidism [Ref O2]. Cardiac complications from sustained hyperthyroidism include left ventricular hypertrophy and dilated cardiomyopathies [Ref O3, O4]. Burn injury produces a hypermetabolic response with marked wasting of lean body mass within weeks, prolonged generalized weakness, increased cardiac work, and elevated resting energy expenditure that profoundly delays recovery [Ref O5]. The hypermetabolic response increases with the magnitude of the metabolic stressor and produces upregulation of catabolic agents (cortisol, catecholamines, glucagon) that drive a hyperdynamic cardiovascular response [Ref O5]."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Behavior. ", bold: true }, "The architecture must throttle its own draw on the mitochondrial pathway regardless of substrate energy needs once Floor 3 is reached. The AI reduces metabolic up-regulation signaling toward zero, accepts that current production will fall, and prepares for transition to Floor 4 with emergency operational behavior. The host begins to recover from the metabolic strain over a recovery period of days to weeks depending on the duration and intensity of the up-regulation that produced Floor 3."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Recovery. ", bold: true }, "Recovery from Floor 3 requires the host to recover from the metabolic strain first, before the architecture can rely on the mitochondrial pathway again. Storage tier states during recovery depend on whether other production sources (piezoelectric, wireless) become available; if not, the architecture descends to Floor 4 while host metabolic recovery proceeds."]));

children.push(H3("3.5 Floor 4: Substrate Maintenance Threshold"));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Definition. ", bold: true }, "Energy is insufficient to maintain the operational integrity of the substrate components themselves. TIF cells require ongoing energy for maintenance, division, and continued cytokine production. Sensor cells require power for reporter circuits. Storage components have minimum maintenance current. Biosynthetically deposited components require biological maintenance \u2014 surrounding cells producing fresh recognition elements as old ones degrade, repair of structural damage, geometric adjustment as bone remodels [Addendum I §3]. All of this requires baseline energy."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Detection. ", bold: true }, "Channel A1 telemetry detects declining TIF cell counts through cytokine signature. Channel A2 detects reduced overall substrate electrical activity. Storage tiers approach minimum maintenance levels."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Behavior. ", bold: true }, "Below Floor 4, substrate components begin degrading: cells die without adequate replacement, sensors lose calibration, storage capacity shrinks as deposited components are not maintained against bone remodeling and immune turnover. The AI enters emergency mode with the following priorities, in order: (1) preserve immune-control infrastructure (Addendum I §2) to prevent rejection cascade against existing components; (2) preserve TIF cell population through any remaining metabolic capacity; (3) signal the host through whatever Channel B-write capability exists or through cytokine effects the host will perceive (hunger, fatigue, urge to seek warmth, urge to seek charging infrastructure) that the substrate is approaching critical state; (4) reduce all other operations to absolute minimum."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Recovery. ", bold: true }, "Days to weeks with active rebuilding once power is restored, depending on how far below the threshold the system descended and for how long. Cells must be replaced through normal cellular replication, sensors must be recalibrated, deposited structures must be repaired or rebuilt where degradation occurred. Recovery is possible from this floor but is not instantaneous; restored operation is incremental as components are restored."]));

children.push(H3("3.6 Floor 5: Critical Failure Threshold"));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Definition. ", bold: true }, "The point below which the substrate cannot recover even if power is restored, because the components required to use restored power have themselves degraded too far. TIF cell population has crashed below the level needed to respond to BphS/MrkH commands. Receiver coils are damaged or absorbed. Storage components have lost capacity through cellular death. The integration has effectively fallen out of the host as a coordinated system, even if some individual deposited components remain."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Detection. ", bold: true }, "Channel A1 telemetry returns null or near-null cytokine signatures from TIF cells. Channel A2 detects no substrate electrical activity at most nodes. The AI cannot direct operations at receiver, storage, or sensor nodes because the cellular infrastructure to receive and execute commands is no longer present."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Behavior without failsafe maintenance subsystem. ", bold: true }, "The integration is lost. Re-deployment from stage one (per v3.2 §2) is required to restore architecture. Existing deposited components that remain in tissue are no longer under active immune control (Addendum I §2) and may begin to attract foreign-body response, potentially producing chronic inflammation or tissue damage at integration sites. This was the architectural failure mode that motivated the development of the failsafe maintenance subsystem specified in §4 of this addendum."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Behavior with failsafe maintenance subsystem. ", bold: true }, "Floor 5 transitions to a documented dormancy state from which recovery is possible indefinitely, as specified in §4. The maintenance subsystem operates independently of the AI layer and continues to preserve integration viability."]));

children.push(H3("3.7 Failure Mode Engineering Discipline"));
children.push(P("The operational floor architecture follows the discipline established in safety-critical embedded systems engineering. Watchdog timers and similar fail-safe mechanisms are pervasive hardware features in safety-critical microcontrollers and are identified in the engineering literature as essential design elements rather than optional refinements [Ref N1, N2]. Implantable medical devices specifically use watchdog units that maintain stay-alive timers and revert devices to safe pre-programmed states when communication or operational integrity is lost [Ref P1, P2, P3]. The configurable FPGA-based watchdog timer architecture \u2014 separate clocks, timed-sequence configuration updates, redundant fault coverage \u2014 is established for safety-critical applications including automotive, aerospace, and medical devices [Ref N3]. The principle of redundancy through external watchdogs that operate independently of the monitored system, rather than relying solely on internal mechanisms that might fail during critical moments, is foundational [Ref N2]."));
children.push(P("The Braided Mind architecture extends these principles by requiring not only watchdog functionality at the AI layer but a separate maintenance subsystem (§4) that operates without AI direction at all. This is a stronger commitment than conventional medical device fail-safe architecture because the substrate's complexity requires preservation of integration during AI-layer failures that might exceed the recovery capability of conventional watchdog-and-revert architectures."));

children.push(H3("3.8 Inter-Floor Transitions"));
children.push(P("The floor architecture is designed for stepwise transitions with documented behavior at each level. Transitions are not sharp thresholds but operational states with defined entry and exit criteria. Multiple floors may be active in different respects simultaneously \u2014 for example, storage may be at Floor 1 (exhausted) while production is at Floor 2 (deficit) and the metabolic pathway has not yet been engaged. The AI tracks these states independently and selects behavior based on the highest-priority active floor."));
children.push(P("The architecture commits to the following transition triggers:"));
children.push(Bullet("Floor 0 (normal operation) \u2192 Floor 1: Tier 1 and Tier 2 storage drop below 10% of capacity."));
children.push(Bullet("Floor 1 \u2192 Floor 2: Tier 3 storage begins drawing down, indicating sustained production deficit."));
children.push(Bullet("Floor 2 \u2192 Floor 3: Mitochondrial source duration approaches host-specific safe limit (per §1.5.5)."));
children.push(Bullet("Floor 3 \u2192 Floor 4: Substrate component maintenance current drops below sustainable level."));
children.push(Bullet("Floor 4 \u2192 Floor 5: Component degradation crosses irreversibility threshold without active intervention."));
children.push(P("The specific numerical thresholds for each transition require empirical determination prior to clinical deployment and are identified as open parameters requiring per-host calibration."));

children.push(HR());

// ============= Section 4 =============
children.push(H1("4. Failsafe Maintenance Subsystem"));

children.push(H3("4.1 Architectural Rationale"));
children.push(P("The operational floor analysis in §3 identifies Floor 5 as the irreversible failure threshold under the architecture as specified in v3.2 and Addendum I. Recovery from Floor 5 requires re-deployment from stage one of the original protocol [v3.2 §2], and during the descent from Floor 4 to Floor 5 the existing deposited components lose immune control [Addendum I §2] and may produce chronic inflammation or tissue damage at integration sites."));
children.push(P("This failure mode is unacceptable as a final state of the architecture. The principle drawn from safety-critical systems engineering is that complex systems require redundancy through subsystems that operate independently of the primary control layer, rather than relying on internal mechanisms that might fail during critical moments [§3 Ref N2]. Implantable medical device watchdog architectures address this through external watchdog units that maintain stay-alive timers and revert devices to safe pre-programmed states when communication or operational integrity is lost [§3 Ref P1, P2, P3]. The Braided Mind architecture extends this principle by requiring not only watchdog functionality but a separate maintenance subsystem that operates without AI direction at all."));
children.push(P("Section 4 specifies this subsystem. The design principle is that the maintenance layer cannot fail in the same ways the AI layer can fail, because it has no decision logic, no AI compute, no power requirements beyond cellular metabolism, and no communication channel that can be jammed. It runs as long as cells run, which is as long as the host is alive."));

children.push(H3("4.2 Design Principle: Cellular Logic Without AI Compute"));
children.push(P("The maintenance subsystem is implemented as gene-circuit logic in dedicated maintenance cell populations distributed through the same biosynthetic deployment infrastructure specified in Addendum I §3. Synthetic biology has established the building blocks for cell-autonomous logic operating purely through transcriptional regulation without computational layer above."));
children.push(mix([
  Pt("The genetic toggle switch demonstrated by Gardner, Cantor, and Collins constructs a synthetic bistable gene-regulatory network in "),
  Pt("E. coli", { italics: true }),
  Pt(" from any two repressible promoters arranged in a mutually inhibitory network [Ref Q1]. The toggle is flipped between stable states using transient chemical or thermal induction and exhibits a nearly ideal switching threshold, forming a synthetic addressable cellular memory unit with implications for biotechnology and gene therapy [Ref Q1]. Subsequent work has extended this to tristable switches that maintain three stable states from repressible promoters [Ref Q2] and has demonstrated toggle switch function in plants and other organisms [Ref Q3]."),
]));
children.push(mix([
  Pt("The Elowitz-Leibler repressilator demonstrated synthetic oscillatory networks in "),
  Pt("E. coli", { italics: true }),
  Pt(" using three transcriptional repressor systems not part of any natural biological clock, arranged in a cyclic negative-feedback loop where each gene represses transcription of the next [Ref Q4]. The network periodically induces synthesis of fluorescent reporter protein with characteristic period of hours, slower than the cell-division cycle, and demonstrates that engineered oscillatory behavior can be transmitted across generations [Ref Q4]. Subsequent work has refined the repressilator with enhanced versions that buffer stochasticity and entrain to external signals [Ref Q5], and has demonstrated cyclic gene-circuit oscillators across diverse synthetic biology contexts [Ref Q6]."),
]));
children.push(P("These foundational constructs establish that cells can be programmed with stable memory states, oscillatory cycles, and threshold-triggered transitions through transcriptional logic alone, without any external compute layer. The Braided Mind maintenance subsystem combines these primitives into a single-purpose preservation circuit."));

children.push(H3("4.3 Function Set"));
children.push(P("The maintenance subsystem performs four functions only. It has no decision logic beyond these four; the architecture deliberately constrains its capability to prevent it from being able to do harm in novel ways."));

children.push(mix([{ text: "Function 1: Maintain TIF cell population through scheduled antigen restimulation. ", bold: true }, "Stage one of the original v3.2 deployment is repeated on a fixed cycle through maintenance cells that produce the antigen at scheduled intervals (cycle period on order of months, established empirically per host). The mechanism uses a synthetic-biology oscillator analogous to the repressilator [Ref Q4] tuned to multi-month period rather than the original short-period demonstration. TIF cells continue producing baseline immune-control cytokines through constitutive low-level expression that does not require BphS/MrkH activation \u2014 sufficient to prevent rejection of existing deposited components but lower-bandwidth than the AI-directed modulation specified in v3.2 §4."]));

children.push(mix([{ text: "Function 2: Maintain biocompatibility of deposited components through baseline Treg recruitment to integration sites. ", bold: true }, "Constitutive expression of TGF-\u03B2 and IL-10 from maintenance cells co-located with each major substrate node provides baseline tolerance maintenance. The cytokine biology is well-established: TGF-\u03B2 and IL-10 are the principal cytokines mediating peripheral T-cell tolerance through induction of regulatory T cell populations [Ref R1, R2]. TGF-\u03B2 promotes naive T cell differentiation into induced Treg cells (iTregs) through TGF-\u03B2 receptor signaling and Smad4-dependent FoxP3 induction [Ref R3, R4]. IL-10 regulates at the interface of innate and adaptive immunity to limit the magnitude of immune responses [Ref R3]. Type 1 regulatory T (Tr1) cells produce high levels of both IL-10 and TGF-\u03B2 and mediate suppression of pathological immune responses in transplantation, allergy, and autoimmune contexts [Ref R5]. The combination of TGF-\u03B2 and IL-10 cooperates in mucosal tolerance and in the response to environmental antigens [Ref R1, R6]."]));
children.push(P("The architectural application is constitutive production from maintenance cells, not AI-modulated production. The maintenance subsystem cannot adjust cytokine output in response to changing conditions, cannot selectively tolerate or reject specific structures, cannot adapt to context. It runs the baseline tolerance state at fixed levels that prevent inflammatory response against existing deposited components without preventing normal immune function for everything else. Loss of fine modulation is acceptable because the function being served is preservation, not active immune management."));

children.push(mix([{ text: "Function 3: Preserve structural integrity of biosynthesized components through scheduled remodeling. ", bold: true }, "Bone undergoes natural remodeling through osteoblast/osteoclast balance throughout life, and the deposited structures specified in Addendum I §3 must be maintained against this remodeling or they progressively erode. Maintenance cells direct localized osteoblast activity to repair structural deposits on a slow cycle, using bistable gene-circuit logic [Ref Q1] to track which sites need maintenance and to coordinate the repair sequence. The maintenance does not add new function or upgrade existing structures; it preserves what exists from being absorbed by the host's normal bone turnover."]));

children.push(mix([{ text: "Function 4: Heartbeat signal for AI restoration detection. ", bold: true }, "A simple periodic cytokine pulse or marker expression that any restored AI substrate will recognize as \u201Cmaintenance subsystem operational, integration preserved, ready for AI rebuild.\u201D The signal uses a low-frequency oscillator (period on order of hours) implemented through standard synthetic biology oscillator constructs [Ref Q4, Q5]. When the AI layer is restored through subsequent intervention, the heartbeat signal is the first indication that the underlying integration is intact and the AI can re-establish higher-order control on existing infrastructure rather than starting from stage one."]));

children.push(H3("4.4 Why This Cannot Fail in the Same Modes as the AI Layer"));
children.push(P("The maintenance subsystem operates without:"));
children.push(Bullet([
  Pt("AI compute. ", { bold: true }),
  Pt("No transformer-architecture model, no neural network operations, no decision logic that depends on substrate processing nodes being functional. The subsystem's behavior is determined entirely by transcriptional regulation in maintenance cells."),
]));
children.push(Bullet([
  Pt("Communication beyond cell-local cytokine signaling. ", { bold: true }),
  Pt("The subsystem does not require BphS/MrkH NIR command channels, does not depend on substrate electrical network function, does not communicate with processing nodes. Each maintenance cell operates independently based on its own internal gene-circuit state."),
]));
children.push(Bullet([
  Pt("External power beyond cellular metabolism. ", { bold: true }),
  Pt("The subsystem draws on the same metabolic ATP that any cell uses for normal operation. As long as the host is alive and providing baseline circulation and nutrition to the maintenance cells, the subsystem operates. There is no additional power requirement from the substrate's energy management infrastructure."),
]));
children.push(Bullet([
  Pt("Storage state. ", { bold: true }),
  Pt("The subsystem does not draw on the storage tiers specified in §2 of this addendum. Storage state at any tier is irrelevant to maintenance subsystem operation."),
]));
children.push(Bullet([
  Pt("Sensor input. ", { bold: true }),
  Pt("The subsystem does not require Channel A telemetry [Addendum I §1] or any other input to operate. It runs its fixed cycle regardless of substrate condition or host state."),
]));
children.push(P("This isolation is the central architectural commitment. The maintenance subsystem and the AI layer share no critical dependencies. Whatever fails in the AI layer cannot propagate to the maintenance layer because the maintenance layer does not depend on anything the AI layer provides."));

children.push(H3("4.5 Inability to Cause Novel Harm"));
children.push(P("A common failure mode in safety-critical fail-safe designs is that the fail-safe layer, given enough capability to be useful, also has enough capability to fail in novel ways. The Braided Mind maintenance subsystem addresses this by deliberately constraining its capability set to the four functions in §4.3."));
children.push(P("The full AI layer can theoretically misdirect the immune system, drive harmful cytokine cascades, deplete the host metabolically, or cause damage in ways that depend on the AI's flexibility and decision-making capacity. The maintenance layer has none of those capabilities \u2014 it cannot drive variable cytokine cascades because it produces constitutive output only, it cannot deplete the host because it has no metabolic up-regulation pathway, and it cannot direct novel actions because it has no decision logic at all. Its harm potential is bounded by what its four functions can do in their fixed-cycle operation."));
children.push(P("This bounding is not a limitation of capability but a deliberate design feature. The maintenance layer is built to be unable to do more than preserve, so that its operation can be trusted unconditionally. If the maintenance layer could do more, it would also be able to fail in ways that would require additional fail-safe layers to detect and correct."));

children.push(H3("4.6 Subordination to AI Layer During Normal Operation"));
children.push(P("When the AI layer is functioning normally, the maintenance subsystem's outputs are subordinate to AI direction. The constitutive Treg recruitment from maintenance cells is overridden by the AI's more sophisticated targeted modulation [Addendum I §2]. The scheduled antigen restimulation is paused if the AI is maintaining TIF populations through other means. The structural integrity maintenance happens beneath the AI's higher-tier remodeling [Addendum I §3]."));
children.push(P("The override mechanism uses dominant-negative regulation: when the AI directs higher-output cytokine signaling through TIF cells under BphS/MrkH command, the elevated cytokine concentration overrides the maintenance subsystem's baseline output without requiring direct communication with maintenance cells. The maintenance cells continue producing their baseline output; the AI's elevated output simply dominates the local cytokine environment. When the AI's elevated output ceases, the baseline output resumes its effective role."));
children.push(P("This subordination preserves the maintenance subsystem's independence. The maintenance cells do not receive commands from the AI layer; they continue running their fixed cycle regardless. The AI's presence simply produces a stronger signal that dominates the integrated tissue response. The AI's absence returns control to the maintenance baseline without the maintenance subsystem needing to detect or respond to the change."));

children.push(H3("4.7 Consequences for Operational Floor Architecture"));
children.push(P("With the failsafe maintenance subsystem in place, the operational floor architecture from §3 changes meaningfully:"));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Floor 4 modification. ", bold: true }, "When substrate maintenance threshold is reached, the maintenance subsystem continues operating because it does not depend on substrate energy availability. TIF cell population is preserved through scheduled antigen restimulation. Existing deposited components remain biocompatible through constitutive Treg recruitment. Structural integrity is preserved through scheduled remodeling. The substrate's AI layer fails, but the integration as a structural and immunological state is preserved."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "Floor 5 elimination as permanent state. ", bold: true }, "The previous Floor 5 definition \u2014 irreversible loss of integration \u2014 no longer applies. What was Floor 5 becomes \u201CAI dormancy\u201D \u2014 a state in which the AI layer is offline but the substrate infrastructure remains intact and the maintenance subsystem holds the integration at preservation baseline. Recovery from AI dormancy is possible indefinitely as long as the host remains alive and the maintenance subsystem continues operating."]));
children.push(mix([{ text: "New ultimate failure mode. ", bold: true }, "The only true irrecoverable failure mode is host death, which terminates cellular metabolism and therefore terminates the maintenance subsystem along with all other biological function. Host death is outside the scope of the architecture; the substrate's design cannot prevent host death, only avoid contributing to it."]));
children.push(P("This is a substantial improvement in the architecture's safety profile. The original architecture had multiple paths to permanent integration loss; the architecture with failsafe maintenance subsystem has one (host death) that the architecture itself cannot affect."));

children.push(H3("4.8 Implementation Considerations"));
children.push(P("The genetic payload for the maintenance subsystem adds approximately 1.5\u20132.5 kb to the stage three payload specified in v3.2 §4. The components include:"));
children.push(Bullet("Tri-cistronic constitutive expression cassette for TGF-\u03B2 and IL-10, with cell-type-specific promoter for bone-resident maintenance cell populations: ~1.5 kb"));
children.push(Bullet("Slow oscillator for antigen restimulation cycle (multi-month period through engineered repressilator-class architecture per [Ref Q4]): ~0.8 kb"));
children.push(Bullet("Heartbeat oscillator for AI restoration detection: ~0.4 kb (overlapping with antigen restimulation oscillator components where possible)"));
children.push(Bullet("Bistable toggle switch for structural remodeling state tracking [Ref Q1]: ~0.5 kb"));
children.push(P("Total addition: ~2.5 kb without optimization, ~1.5 kb with shared regulatory components. This addition brings the projected stage three payload from the v3.2-specified 5.8\u20136.5 kb (or 6.5\u20137.2 kb with HO-1 supplementation) to approximately 7.3\u20138.0 kb (without HO-1) or 8.0\u20139.7 kb (with HO-1). The lentiviral packaging limit of 8\u201310 kb [v3.2 ref 7] accommodates this in most configurations but is approached closely. Implementation may require either deferring HO-1 supplementation pending in-vivo confirmation that endogenous biliverdin levels suffice [v3.2 §4.3], or splitting the payload across multiple delivery vectors with staged deployment."));
children.push(P("The maintenance cell population itself is recruited from a subset of bone marrow stromal cells engineered through a separate stage of the deployment, distinct from the TIF cells specified in v3.2 §3. Maintenance cells do not require the BCOR/ZC3H12A knockout that produces the TIF state because they are not effector cells; they are dedicated maintenance cells with their own engineered gene-circuit payload but otherwise standard bone marrow stromal cell biology. This deployment pattern is presented as a proposed architecture requiring experimental validation before clinical deployment."));

children.push(H3("4.9 Relationship to Watchdog Timer Engineering"));
children.push(P("The architectural pattern follows the watchdog timer principle established in safety-critical embedded systems [§3 Ref N1, N2, N3]. Conventional watchdog timers reset the system if no activity is detected from the monitored process, ensuring reliability under software anomalies or hardware failures. FPGA-based watchdog architectures with separate clocks, secure timed-sequence configuration, and dual-stage fault detection extend this principle to safety-critical applications in automotive, aerospace, and medical devices [§3 Ref N3]."));
children.push(P("The Braided Mind maintenance subsystem differs from conventional watchdog timers in three ways:"));
children.push(Bullet([
  Pt("Continuous operation rather than reset trigger. ", { bold: true }),
  Pt("Conventional watchdogs fire only when their timer expires and trigger system reset. The maintenance subsystem operates continuously regardless of the AI layer's state, providing baseline preservation rather than resetting a failed system."),
]));
children.push(Bullet([
  Pt("Subordination rather than override during normal operation. ", { bold: true }),
  Pt("Conventional watchdogs do not affect normal operation; they are silent until they fire. The maintenance subsystem produces continuous output that is overridden by the AI layer's higher-output operation during normal function, providing baseline behavior even when the AI is operating normally."),
]));
children.push(Bullet([
  Pt("No reset capability. ", { bold: true }),
  Pt("Conventional watchdogs reset systems to known-good states. The maintenance subsystem cannot reset the AI layer because it has no compute capability and no command channel. Recovery from AI dormancy requires external intervention (re-deployment of AI components) rather than autonomous reset."),
]));
children.push(P("The differences reflect the different operational context. Conventional medical devices have well-defined safe states they can revert to and external programming hardware that can rebuild lost state quickly. The Braided Mind architecture has neither \u2014 there is no simple \u201Csafe state\u201D to revert the AI to, and rebuilding lost AI capability requires biological redeployment that takes time. The maintenance subsystem accordingly provides preservation rather than restoration, holding the integration intact while external intervention rebuilds the AI layer over the timescale required."));

children.push(HR());

// ============= Section 5 limitations =============
children.push(H1("5. Limitations and Open Questions"));
children.push(P("In addition to the limitations enumerated in v3.2 §9 and Addendum I §4, this addendum introduces the following:"));
children.push(Bullet("The four-source energy generation architecture requires per-host calibration of switching thresholds, mitochondrial up-regulation safe duration limits, and storage tier sizing. These are identified as open parameters requiring empirical determination prior to clinical deployment."));
children.push(Bullet("Microbial extracellular electron transfer machinery has not been demonstrated in mammalian tissue under AI-directed control. The biology is established in bacterial systems; the integration into mammalian bone marrow with eukaryotic cellular machinery is novel and requires demonstration."));
children.push(Bullet("Biosynthesized capacitor and pseudocapacitor structures have not been demonstrated in vivo. The constituent biology (hydroxyapatite biomineralization, ferritin-templated iron oxide synthesis) and the constituent electrochemistry (HA dielectric function, iron oxide pseudocapacitor function) are established independently. The combined deployment requires experimental development."));
children.push(Bullet("Multi-heme cytochrome arrays as deep storage have not been demonstrated as a structured deployment in mammalian tissue. The MHC bioelectronic conductivity is established in solid-state junctions; the array architecture for energy storage is novel."));
children.push(Bullet("Wireless charging coil deployment through biosynthetic infrastructure requires demonstration. The charging technology and the biosynthetic capability are independently established; the integration is novel."));
children.push(Bullet("Thermal safety architecture parameters require per-host calibration against individual perfusion characteristics. The Pennes bioheat framework is established for thermal modeling; specific deployment parameters are open."));
children.push(Bullet("The operational floor transitions specify trigger conditions but require empirical determination of numerical thresholds. Per-host calibration is required."));
children.push(Bullet("The failsafe maintenance subsystem combines established synthetic biology primitives (toggle switch, repressilator-class oscillators, constitutive cytokine expression) into a novel preservation architecture. Each primitive is independently demonstrated; the combined architecture is novel and requires validation."));
children.push(Bullet("Lentiviral packaging capacity is approached closely with the full Addendum I and Addendum II deployment. Multi-vector delivery may be required in practice."));

children.push(HR());

